    <h1>The Natural Law Resurgence Movement</h1>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap narrow-wrap prose-page movement">
    <p>The annual event devoted to &ldquo;Natural Law and the Renewal of Western Civilization,&rdquo; in Washington D.C., forms part of a greater effort coined the &ldquo;Natural Law Resurgence Movement&rdquo;: a worldwide coordinated endeavor dedicated to the natural law (research, study, discussion, publication, formation, teaching).</p>
    <p>Scholars, many of whom maintain close working relationships with politicians and state leaders, have been gathering together to advance serious reflection on the general principles of morality that give fundamental purpose to: 1) man as a social being with a natural aptitude for political society and a compelling need for it; 2) the family based on marriage between one man and woman, the first natural society: school of love and virtue ethics; and 3) the State, also a natural society that offers the means to the common good of men, at the national level, which has a plenitude of sovereignty for international relationship with other nation states.</p>
    <p>The movement seeks not primarily to settle theoretical disagreements among competing schools of natural law, but to recover a shared foundation for moral reasoning and public discourse, particularly in response to contemporary challenges in social, political and legal life to the existence of objective truth and the capacity of human reason to know it and to accurately assess and limit unjust laws.</p>
    <p>Important contributions to the movement are happening at conference venues, publishing houses, and academic institutions with coordination among many actors and entities. Notable organizations involved in the movement for the purposes of the event, &ldquo;Natural Law and the Renewal of Western Civilization,&rdquo; include</p>
    <ul>
      <li><i>Lumen de Lumine</i> Foundation: promotes the activities of canonist and natural law expert, Cardinal Raymond Leo Burke;</li>
      <li><a href="https://icjurist.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">International Catholic Jurists Forum</a>: co-organizes natural law activities, conferences and publications with other entities;</li>
      <li><a href="https://fundacionveritas.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><i>Fundación Véritas</i></a>: is sponsoring the creation of a center devoted to natural law and related programs at the <i>Universidad del Medio Atlántico</i> (UNAM), in the Canary Islands, Spain;</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.iurisnaturalis.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><i>Iuris Naturalis Societas</i></a>: organizes activities and shares information and materials about the natural law with its numerous group of members spread throughout the world, many of whom are professors of legal philosophy; and</li>
      <li><a href="https://subsidium.online/author.php?slug=iain-benson" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><i>Subsidium</i></a>: considers many issues especially ones of international significance through the lens of the principle of subsidiarity.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Notable examples of recent activities under the umbrella of the &ldquo;Natural Law Resurgence Movement,&rdquo; include the 2026 conference held in Rome on the thought of <a href="https://www.iurisnaturalis.com/ver-ficha/5501" target="_blank" rel="noopener">St. Thomas Aquinas</a>; the 18<sup>th</sup> consecutive conference held in Central and South America, namely the 2026 <a href="https://jornadasderechonatural.com/en" target="_blank" rel="noopener">annual conference</a>, in Santiago, Chile (going forward, the organizers wish to coordinate efforts with Europeans and North Americans); several events in Poland involving leading universities there; and the 1948 Universal Declaration of Human Rights (UDHR) project whereby a group of scholars, many of them qualified lawyers with practical legal experience, considered the original purpose of this foundational document and developed a draft declaration highlighting the UDHR&rsquo;s implicit and explicit natural law foundation.</p>
    <p>From the outset, contributing scholars to the UDHR project viewed it as necessarily imperfect due to the frailties of the diplomatic process, and the necessarily limited historical perspective at the time, where certain matters could be taken for granted or had not yet been identified as problematic. Certainly, there are many contemporary challenges for state leaders, given the rise of &ldquo;gender ideology,&rdquo; which has contributed to the proliferation of &ldquo;new rights,&rdquo; while other anti-life ideologies promote abortion and euthanasia. These concerns stand alongside the ever-expanding categories of &ldquo;discrimination&rdquo; (e.g., &ldquo;sexual orientation&rdquo; and &ldquo;gender identity,&rdquo; hate speech and so forth), which are being used as a hammer to <a href="https://icjurist.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">persecute Christians in the courts.</a> The outcome document has been designated &ldquo;The New York Consensus Declaration&rdquo; (NYCD) to reflect a possible use of it by state leaders during United Nations General Assembly (UNGA) meetings, in New York. An explanation note, largely drafted by the head of <a href="https://subsidium.online/author.php?slug=iain-benson" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><i>Subsidium</i></a>, explains the implicit and explicit natural law foundation in more detail with reference to common challenges and misconceptions.</p>
  </div>
</section>
